\section{Separate the Claims a Pass Makes}

An SSA pass has at least three postconditions: its result is well formed, it preserves the required behavior, and every analysis it reports as preserved still answers valid queries. None of these implies the others. Replacing an instruction by an arbitrary constant can leave types, dominance, and use lists perfect. A legal rewrite can leave stale metadata. And a faulty update to a dominator tree can deceive later passes even when the current IR is entirely correct.

Jianzhou Zhao's \emph{Formalizing an SSA-Based Compiler for Verified Advanced Program Transformations} makes SSA dominance, typing, and operational semantics explicit in the Vellvm work. A practical structural verifier should check unique definitions, type correctness, CFG and terminator agreement, and matching predecessor edge and $\phi$ operand sets. Ordinary operands require dominance at their instruction, including instruction order in the same block. A merge operand instead needs to be available on its incoming edge. If the IR maintains bidirectional def-use links, check those too. Build the verifier's dominance facts from the current CFG, not from the cache whose validity is under test.

\section{A Legal Edge Change Can Invalidate a Legal Answer}

Start with this fragment; \texttt{A} is the only predecessor of \texttt{J}.

\begin{verbatim}
entry: if c then A else K
A:     a = 10; goto J
K:     return 20
J:     return a
\end{verbatim}

To unify the returns, change \texttt{K} to branch to \texttt{J} and make \texttt{J} return a merge with operand \texttt{a} on the edge from \texttt{A} and \texttt{20} on the edge from \texttt{K}. Leaving \texttt{return a} in \texttt{J} would now be invalid. With the merge in place, the rewrite is structurally valid and preserves both return values, but \texttt{A} no longer dominates \texttt{J}. An unchanged set of block identities does not preserve a dominator tree. Test both inputs, check the new operand mapping, and compare a maintained tree against a fresh one. If a pass forgets to update or invalidate the tree, the last check fails even though the first two may succeed.

Declare preservation based on actual edits, not the name of the pass. A pure operand replacement leaves block dominance intact under the same CFG but can invalidate value numbering or liveness. A change to stores or call effects can invalidate memory-dependence answers without changing the scalar graph. Loop information, branch frequencies, and derived analysis results have their own dependencies. Invalidate transitively when clients depend on invalidated results; updater methods for an analysis need to cover all affected operations. A conservative invalidation costs recomputation, while false preservation can miscompile the next pass. The dossier's Chapter~22, \emph{Incremental Compiler as a Reactive System}, extends that distinction to dependency tracking across edits.

\section{Validate Behavior across a Rewrite}

A checker for a local arithmetic rewrite must use the actual dialect semantics. Replacing \texttt{x / x} by \texttt{1} is valid for ordinary signed integers when \texttt{x} is nonzero, but not for zero in a dialect where division traps. More generally a validator may check refinement instead of symmetric equivalence, depending on how the IR models undefined behavior. Specify which outputs, effects, traps, and the relevant termination behavior are observable. A test interpreter can detect differences on concrete inputs; passing tests does not prove refinement.

Silvain Rideau and Xavier Leroy's \emph{Validating Register Allocation and Spilling} illustrates a domain-specific validator: backward dataflow infers required equations between source values and target register or stack locations. If the emitted code establishes those equations at the right points, the validator can accept the result without trusting the allocator's choices. They prove the validator sound for its model. A sound checker need not accept every correct transformation. On rejection or timeout, a compiler that uses validation as a correctness gate needs a trusted alternative or the unchanged program, not permission to ship the unchecked rewrite.

\section{Exercise Each Guarantee Independently}

Maintain tests that deliberately violate each postcondition: a merge operand on the wrong edge for the verifier, division at zero for semantic checking, and the formerly dominating block for preservation checking. Add metamorphic tests which rename SSA values, reorder lists together with their edge maps, or split a legal edge without changing behavior. The dossier's Chapters~30 and~39, \emph{Transactional Compiler Transformations} and \emph{Semantic Fingerprints and Metamorphic Compiler Testing}, suggest staging edits until checks pass and keeping semantic tests independent of the implementation that produced the rewrite.
